  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA broad area of high pressure will remain present over the\nregion throughout this week. Under which, skies will be clear \nto mostly clear, and conditions will be warm and dry. \nTemperatures will max out in the 50s and 60s, though 70 may be \nwithin reach for some valley locations. Large diurnal \ntemperature swings are expected as the lack of cloud cover will \nfavor good radiational cooling. Nightly lows will range from the\n20s to 30s across the region. Terrain will play a role in how \nwarm or cold a location will get. For this afternoon, \ntemperatures in the lower terrain were increased slightly above \nNBM guidance as NBM was low yesterday. Then for tonight, model \nsoundings show another another sharp inversion near the surface,\nso the lower elevations are expected to become cooler than some\nof the higher elevations, similarly to what is being observed \nearly this morning. In addition, patchy valley fog will be \npossible most nights.\n\nAs we continue to see consecutive warm, dry days, we will have to \nmonitor the upcoming week(s) for favorable fire weather conditions. \nRelative humidity could drop below 30% during peak daytime heating \nthough winds will be light most days. Winds do pick up during the \nsecond half of the week before calming by the weekend. As leaves \ncontinue to fall, the increased leaf litter can be fuel for any fire \nthat ignites.",
